Context: this is Section 5 of the notes, 'Spécialisation des MD-graphes'. MD-graphes are Mumford–Deligne graphs, i.e. stable dual graphs of stable pointed curves. An MD-graph G = (S, Ã, σ_Ã, I, Î = Ã ⊔ I → S, g: S → ℕ) has:
- a vertex set S;
- a set Ã of half-edges (Grothendieck's 'arcs'), with a fixed-point-free involution σ_Ã whose orbits are the edges;
- a set I of legs (marked points);
- an incidence map Î → S;
- a genus function g on vertices.

The strata of the product M̂_G = ∏_α M̂_{g_α, Î_α} are indexed by choosing, for each vertex α, an MD-graph G(α) of type (g_α, Î_α), whose leg set is I(α) = Î_α = I_α ⊔ Ã_α. Grothendieck works with the G(α) themselves rather than with isomorphism classes rel Î_α.

This page builds the specialized MD-graph G' by formulas (4_0):
- S' = ⊔_α S(α);
- Ã' = Ã ⊔ ⊔_α Ã(α), with the involution induced by σ_Ã and the σ_{Ã(α)};
- I' = I.

The flag set then decomposes: Î' = Ã' ⊔ I' ≅ (Ã ⊔ I) ⊔ ⊔_α Ã(α). Since Ã ⊔ I = ⊔_α Î_α = ⊔_α I(α), this gives Î' ≅ ⊔_α (I(α) ⊔ Ã(α)) = ⊔_α Î(α). The incidence map Î' → S' and the genus map g' are induced componentwise.

The 'practical recipe': replace each vertex α of G by an MD-graph G(α) of numerical type (g_α, ν̂_α). Here ν̂_α = ν_α + μ_α, with ν_α = card I_α and μ_α the 'order' of α, the number of half-edges of G at α. Equivalently, G(α) has type (g_α, Î_α). The old edges at α are reattached to vertices of G(α) via the legs Ã_α ⊂ I(α).

The result G' specializes to G: contracting all the new edges Ã(α) recovers G. This is exactly vertex substitution (insertion) of stable graphs. It describes how a boundary stratum of M̄_{g,n} indexed by G is closed up by deeper strata, those indexed by refinements G' → G. In other words, it encodes the stratification poset of the Deligne–Mumford boundary, used for the 'structure à l'infini' of M_{g,ν}. The next page confirms the data: connected G(α), genus map, distribution of marked points, new origins for the old arcs, and the stability inequality at genus-0 vertices.
